.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "why", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 1. Why memo_wise Over the ||= Idiom
  :markdown
    The `||=` idiom re-runs the method whenever the cached value is **falsy**.
    A lookup that legitimately returns `nil` or `false` — "does this user
    have a subscription?" — is never memoized at all; it just runs on every
    call while looking cached. The fix is `defined?(@x) ? @x : (@x = …)`,
    which is correct and unreadable. Add a method **argument** and you are
    now hand-maintaining a hash keyed by the arguments. Add a second thread
    and you are reasoning about races.

.mt-6
  %div{ class: "grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2" }
    %div
      = daisy_badge("Before", css: "badge-ghost mb-2")
      = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
        :plain
          def subscription
            # nil is never cached: this runs every call
            @subscription ||= Subscription.find_by(user: user)
          end

          def price_for(plan)
            @price_for ||= {}
            return @price_for[plan] if @price_for.key?(plan)

            @price_for[plan] = PricingEngine.quote(plan, user)
          end
    %div
      = daisy_badge("After", css: "badge-primary mb-2")
      = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
        :plain
          prepend MemoWise

          memo_wise def subscription
            Subscription.find_by(user: user)
          end

          memo_wise def price_for(plan)
            PricingEngine.quote(plan, user)
          end

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    memo_wise gets all of that right — `nil` and `false` are memoized,
    arguments are keyed automatically, class and module methods work, and
    memoized methods are inherited — with **documented thread-safety
    guarantees** (before a value is memoized, concurrent callers may each run
    the method but exactly one result is stored; after, every caller gets
    that result) and reads that
    [benchmark](https://github.com/panorama-ed/memo_wise#benchmarks) faster
    than every other memoization gem. It also gives you first-class
    **reset** and **preset** helpers, which the `||=` idiom has no answer
    for at all.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "install", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 2. Installation
  :markdown
    Add the gem to your `Gemfile` and run `bundle install`. There is no
    generator and nothing to configure.

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # Gemfile
      gem "memo_wise", "~> 1.13"

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "usage", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 3. Usage
  :markdown
    `prepend MemoWise` in the class, then mark methods with `memo_wise`. It
    accepts a method name **or** the `def` itself (since `def` returns the
    method's name as a symbol), so the decorator form reads naturally.
    Memoized methods keep their visibility, so `private` still applies:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # app/models/invoice.rb
      class Invoice < ApplicationRecord
        prepend MemoWise

        # No arguments: computed once per invoice instance.
        memo_wise def total
          line_items.sum { |item| item.quantity * item.unit_price }
        end

        # With arguments: computed once per distinct (currency) value.
        memo_wise def total_in(currency)
          CurrencyConverter.convert(total, from: "USD", to: currency)
        end

        private

        memo_wise def tax_rate
          TaxTable.rate_for(billing_address.region)
        end
      end

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Class methods memoize the same way with the `self:` form. This is the
    natural home for data that is genuinely constant for the life of the
    process, such as a parsed configuration file:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      class FeatureFlags
        prepend MemoWise

        def self.definitions
          YAML.load_file(Rails.root.join("config/feature_flags.yml"))
        end
        memo_wise self: :definitions
      end

= doc_note(modifier: :note, title: "Blocks Can't Be Memoized", css: "mt-6") do
  :markdown
    A method that takes a block — explicit `&block` or an implicit `yield` —
    cannot be memoized, because the block is part of the input and has no
    identity to key on. Restructure so the memoized method takes plain
    values.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "reset", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 4. Resetting and Presetting
  :markdown
    A memoized value is a snapshot, and sometimes the world it was taken
    from changes. `reset_memo_wise` forgets memoized results — for one
    method, for one method with a particular argument, or for everything on
    the object — and `preset_memo_wise` stores a value without running the
    method at all:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      invoice.reset_memo_wise(:total)              # forget total
      invoice.reset_memo_wise(:total_in, "EUR")    # forget one argument's result
      invoice.reset_memo_wise                      # forget everything on this object

      invoice.preset_memo_wise(:total) { 0 }       # store 0 without computing

      FeatureFlags.reset_memo_wise                 # class methods reset the same way

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Two places this matters:

    - **Objects that outlive the data they memoized.** A request-scoped
      object (a component, an interaction, a presenter) is thrown away
      before anything can go stale, so it rarely needs a reset. A model that
      memoizes a derived value and then has a setter called does — reset in
      the setter, or don't memoize the derived value.
    - **Tests that share an instance.** Rails view helpers are modules that
      rspec-rails tests through a single shared `helper` object, so a
      memoized helper method keeps its result from one example to the next.
      Reset between examples:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # spec/helpers/pricing_helper_spec.rb
      RSpec.describe PricingHelper, type: :helper do
        after { helper.reset_memo_wise }
      end

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "when", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 5. When to Memoize — and When Not To
  :markdown
    memo_wise makes memoizing so easy that the discipline has to come from
    you. The test is simple: **the method is pure, it is expensive or called
    repeatedly, and the answer cannot change during the object's lifetime.**
    Miss any one of those and memoization is a bug waiting for the right
    input.

